insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-screening-model-is', 1, 'ISIALA''s screening NPV is 81.0464 million USD. The sampled run at seed 20260829 gives an emv of 80.1707 and a Best case P50 of 81.1835. What is 81.0464?', '["The mean of the sampled NPVs before the engine rounds it for display, which is why it lands so close to the emv of 80.1707.", "The Best case P50 of the deterministic run, the value the project meets or exceeds half of the time.", "The value at the inputs exactly as typed, which is neither a mean nor a Best case P50 of anything.", "What the project will probably earn, since every input is a most likely value."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A deterministic NPV carries no probability. On ISIALA the emv of 80.1707 and the Best case P50 of 81.1835 land close to it, and nothing in the engine promises they will on the next field.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-screening-model-is', 2, 'ISIALA''s Low scenario returns -72.1531 and the sampled run''s Low case P90 is 48.7439. Why are both called low without being the same kind of number?', '["The scenario moves every input the wrong way at once and carries no probability, while 48.7439 is met or exceeded with a 90 percent probability.", "The scenario is a deeper point of the same sampled distribution, taken further into the low tail than the Low case P90, so the two differ only in how much probability they leave below them.", "The Low case P90 is the value the sampled NPV falls below 90 percent of the time, so 48.7439 is the optimistic reading of the run and -72.1531 is the conservative reading of the same run.", "The scenario moves only price and production while the sample moves price, capex and reserves, so the two lows differ because they vary different inputs at the same probability."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A scenario attaches no probability at all, and under the exceedance meaning P90 is a 90 percent probability the NPV meets or exceeds 48.7439. Reading P90 as the value the NPV falls below is the label misread one distractor makes, and the Low scenario also raises capex and fixed opex.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-screening-model-is', 3, 'At ISIALA''s 12 percent discount rate the engine''s 2027 factor is 1.058301 and its 2028 factor is 1.185297. What exponent does it apply to year index i?', '["i, so 2027 is undiscounted and the printed factor belongs to 2028.", "i + 1, the year-end convention, with 1.058301 standing for a stub.", "i + 0.5 in 2027 only, then whole years from 2028 onwards.", "i + 0.5, so even the first year''s cash is discounted by half a year, and every year is discounted half a year less than a year-end model would."]'::jsonb, 3, '(1 + 12/100) raised to 0.5 is 1.058301, and the same base raised to one and a half is 1.185297. That is the mid-year convention, applied to every year of the life.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-screening-model-is', 4, 'A reader sets ISIALA''s screening NPV of 81.0464 beside a Petroleum Economics Studio NPV for the same field and reads the gap as a disagreement about the field. What alone moves ISIALA''s value before either ledger differs in any line?', '["The missing economic limit, since the screening engine keeps late losing years that the Studio would have cut from the same field before valuing it.", "The discounting convention, since the same rows discounted at year end give 76.5817 and the engine reports 81.0464.", "Nothing, once the Studio NPV is multiplied by 1.058301, the factor that converts it into a screening number.", "The tax rate, which one engine reads as a fraction and the other as a percent."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Mid-year against year end moves ISIALA from 76.5817 to 81.0464, a ratio of 1.058301 at 12 percent. The two engines'' numbers are different quantities and are not converted into each other.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-screening-model-is', 5, 'OKPOMA''s screening NPV is 167.4389 million USD. In 2046 its gross revenue is 4.4603 and its opex 4.5718. What does 167.4389 value?', '["The years through 2045, since the engine stops producing a field once its opex first exceeds its gross revenue.", "All twenty years with the 2046 loss floored at zero, since a losing year cannot reduce a positive NPV.", "The years a real operator would produce, which is what a screening NPV reports.", "All twenty years, including 2046''s net cash flow of -0.5576, because the engine has no economic limit."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every year of the life is produced and charged, so -0.5576 sits inside 167.4389. A screening NPV says whether a case deserves a proper model, and the proper model is where an economic limit belongs.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-screening-model-is', 6, 'Which quick input would give ISIALA a life other than 2027 to 2046?', '["None; the quick life is always twenty years.", "The decline rate, since the case ends in the year the volume can no longer carry the fixed opex charged against it.", "The capex, since the engine sizes the life so that a larger development spend has enough years to be recovered.", "The initial rate, since the life is derived from the reserves that 4400 bopd declining at 12 percent a year implies."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The quick form always builds a 20 year case, and ISIALA still produces 141552.0984 bbl in 2046 for a net cash flow of 2.6534. The life is not derived from reserves and no quick input moves it.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-screening-model-is', 7, 'Every ISIALA row carries a gas price of 3.5000. What does that column add to the 2027 grossRevenue of 112.4200?', '["3.5000 million USD a year, a flat gas stream the quick form books beside the oil in every one of the twenty rows.", "A gas stream priced at 3.5000 on the oil volume, which is why grossRevenue runs higher than the oil price alone would give.", "Nothing, because gas volume is 0 in every row and 112.4200 is 1606000.0000 bbl at 70.0000 divided by 1e6.", "Nothing in 2027, since gas starts in 2029 after the capex years."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The gas column exists and contributes nothing: the quick form always sets gas volume to 0 at a gas price of 3.5, so every row is oil alone.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-screening-model-is', 8, 'ISIALA''s 2027 govTake reads 16.8630, the same number as its royalty. What is govTake, and why does it equal the royalty that year?', '["A percentage of gross revenue, 15 in every year.", "Royalty plus tax, in million USD; no tax was due in 2027, so the royalty is all of it.", "The government''s share of net cash flow.", "Royalty alone; tax is reported elsewhere."]'::jsonb, 1, 'In 2029 tax starts and govTake becomes 32.4247, which is royalty of 13.0587 plus tax of 19.3660, all in million USD.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-screening-model-is', 9, 'OKPOMA is typed as 6800 bopd at 78 USD per bbl, and its 2027 grossRevenue reads 193.5960. How did 6800 become that number?', '["The daily rate over a whole year, with no decline taken yet, times 78 and divided by 1e6 into million USD.", "The daily rate times 78, a day''s revenue the ledger stores in USD.", "The daily rate declined by 18 percent, priced at 78, over 1e6.", "The daily rate over a whole year times 78, with the 1e6 applied only to the cost lines so that revenue and opex can share one column."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Production goes from bopd to bbl a year before it is priced, the first year carries no decline, and money is volume times price divided by 1e6. ISIALA''s 1606000.0000 bbl at 70.0000 gives 112.4200 the same way.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-screening-model-is', 10, 'The Probabilistic Breakeven Analyzer states ISIALA''s efficiency belief as a 10th percentile of 85, a median of 91 and a 90th percentile of 96, and runs its base case at 0.91. A reader types 91 where 0.91 belongs. What happens?', '["The engine divides any efficiency above 1 by 100 before it is used, so the case is unchanged.", "The run is refused with a units error, because an efficiency of 91 would multiply every barrel far beyond what the field can produce.", "Nothing changes in the base case, since the base runs at the median belief and 91 is that median.", "The field has changed and nothing complains, because 91 is a belief in percent and 0.91 is the fraction the case runs on."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Units carried from one engine into another fail silently: 91 is the median in percent, and the base case multiplies its barrels by 0.91.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-screening-model-is', 11, 'The breakeven engine runs ISIALA with opex of 20 million USD a year, flat. The screening case uses 2.5 million USD fixed plus 13 USD per bbl. How do the two relate?', '["20 is the screening case''s first year ledger opex rounded down, so the two engines charge the same cost in 2027 and part only later.", "20 is the median of an opex belief, and the screening engine takes that median as its fixed plus variable total once the decline is applied.", "They belong to different cases, and neither is an input to the other.", "The screening case''s two halves add up to less than 20 in every year, so the Analyzer charges the higher cost and nothing else separates the cases."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The screening case builds ledger opex of 23.3780 in 2027 from its two halves and 4.3402 in 2046, while the Analyzer charges a flat 20. A number carried between them changes the field.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-screening-model-is', 12, 'A grossRevenue built from bbl and USD per bbl without dividing by 1e6 is wrong in which way?', '["Too small by a year of days.", "A million times too large, and every royalty computed from it is a million times too large as well, since royalty is taken off that line.", "Unchanged; the engine reads money in USD.", "A thousand times too large, since the ledger is in thousands."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Money is volume times price divided by 1e6, so the hand case''s 1 million bbl a year at 100 USD per bbl reads 100.0000. Without the 1e6 every column built on grossRevenue inherits the error.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-screening-model-is', 13, 'ISIALA''s oil price is 70.0000 in 2027 and 70.0000 in 2046. What does the screening engine say about those two numbers?', '["The 2046 price is in 2027 money, and the 12 percent discount rate turns it into a nominal price.", "The 2046 price is escalated inside the ledger by the discount rate, so only the case table shows it flat.", "They are real prices at a stated base year.", "They are the same number of USD, since the engine has no inflation basis and no currency year."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The units refuse a currency year. There is no inflation basis in the screening engine, so a flat 70.0000 in 2046 is the same number of USD as in 2027.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-screening-model-is', 14, 'An analyst wants to screen ISIALA as a production sharing contract with PIA terms using the quick form. What will the quick form build?', '["A TaxRoyalty case, as always; a PSC exists in the full engine and is never produced by the quick form, and PIA terms live in Petroleum Economics Studio.", "A PSC, once fiscalType is changed on the form, with cost oil taken from the capex split.", "A TaxRoyalty case with the royalty raised to stand in for profit oil.", "A PSC with PIA terms applied, since those are the engine''s default fiscal terms for a field first producing in 2027 and the quick form reads the first year to choose them for the case."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The quick form builds TaxRoyalty only, with no PIA terms, no cost oil, no working interest and no inflation basis; ISIALA''s expanded case reads fiscalType TaxRoyalty.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-screening-model-is', 15, 'From ISIALA''s quick inputs alone, what does the engine report about an oil price 0.7 times the 70 USD per bbl assumption?', '["A warning beside the NPV, since the engine flags any input whose 0.7 case turns the project negative.", "A range printed with the NPV, because the quick form attaches a spread of 20 percent to every price it builds.", "Nothing, until a sweep is asked for.", "An IRR of 53.7148 percent, which already tells a reader how far the price can fall before the NPV reaches zero."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The quick inputs carry no range, so the range has to be asked for and stated beside the number. runSensitivityAnalysis then returns -17.3893 at a price 0.7 times the assumption.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-case-before-the-numbers', 1, 'A reviewer checks ISIALA''s royaltyRate 15, taxRate 35 and discountRate 12 against the quick form, finds them right, and signs off. What has the review missed?', '["Nothing of substance, since the passthrough fields are the only values expandQuickInputs does not recompute and the arrays follow from them mechanically.", "The decisions sitting in the arrays: a 20 year life, TaxRoyalty, gas volume 0 at 3.5, capex split 50/50 and no abandonment.", "The discount factors, which the form computes from the discount rate at year end and which can therefore differ from the rate a reviewer checked.", "Only the first year, the one value the engine may move."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Five things in the case were never asked for and all of them shape the ledger. A reviewer who never opens the arrays never sees that capex landed in production years or that OKPOMA''s 2046 is produced at a loss.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-case-before-the-numbers', 2, 'Where does a reserves figure enter ISIALA''s quick case?', '["As the input that sets the life.", "As a cap on cumulative production.", "Through the initial rate field.", "Nowhere; there is no reserves field, and volume is whatever 4400 bopd declining 12 percent a year adds up to over twenty years."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Volume comes from an initial rate and a decline, so changing the life would change the reserves, and the quick form will not let the life change.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-case-before-the-numbers', 3, 'OKPOMA''s 2046 row has gross revenue 4.4603, opex 4.5718 and net cash flow -0.5576. Which quick input would stop that year being produced?', '["None; every quick case gets twenty rows.", "The decline rate, since a steeper decline brings the end of the life forward to the year the volume stops covering opex.", "The fixed opex, since the engine ends the life at the first year whose opex exceeds gross revenue and a lower figure moves that year.", "The initial rate, since a lower rate shortens the life to the years that the field can pay for."]'::jsonb, 0, 'No quick input moves the life. A field whose economics end early and a field that could run for decades both get twenty rows, so a case that must stop earlier has to be built year by year.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-case-before-the-numbers', 4, 'A reader applies ISIALA''s 12 percent decline before booking 2027 and writes 1413280.0000 bbl in that year. What has happened to the case?', '["Nothing that matters, since the decline is taken once and the other nineteen years are unaffected.", "The case is correct, since the engine also declines its first year and books 1606000.0000 bbl only as a reference.", "2027 now holds the engine''s 2028 volume, every row slides a year early, and the NPV falls for a reason that has nothing to do with the field.", "Only the 2027 revenue is wrong, because each later year is declined from the engine''s own 2027 volume and the error never travels down the column into 2028 and the years after it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The first year is undeclined: 4400 bopd over a full year is 1606000.0000 bbl, and the first reduction appears in 2028.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-case-before-the-numbers', 5, 'ISIALA''s 2046 volume is 0.088140 of its 2027 volume. Why is it not zero after twenty years of a 12 percent decline?', '["Each year is 0.880000 of the year before, so the barrels lost shrink along with the field and the profile never reaches zero.", "The engine floors the volume at an economic limit, and 141552.0984 bbl is the smallest yearly volume it lets a quick case produce.", "The decline stops at year 10, so the second half of the life holds its volume.", "Twelve percent of the 2027 volume is taken away each year, and twenty such steps fall just short of emptying the field entirely."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Taking 12 percent of the 2027 volume away every year would run ISIALA dry well inside twenty years. The engine takes 12 percent of the year before, which is one exponential from the first row to the last.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-case-before-the-numbers', 6, 'ISIALA spends 180 million USD of capex, yet its cumulative bottoms out at -44.6035 in 2028. Why so shallow?', '["Each negative tax base in the capex years earns a refund, which offsets most of the spend before the cumulative is read.", "Oil flows in both capex years, and that revenue has already paid for most of the 180.", "maxExposure is read from discounted cash flows, so the 2028 cumulative is shrunk by the mid-year factor before it is reported.", "Only the first 90.0000 counts toward exposure, since the second half is spent in a year the field already covers from its own revenue."]'::jsonb, 1, 'There is no development year: 2027 produces 1606000.0000 bbl while half the capex is spent, so the cumulative reads -17.8210 and then -44.6035, which is the engine''s maxExposure.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-case-before-the-numbers', 7, 'OKPOMA''s cumulative is 9.2498 after 2027 and -2.2287 after 2028, and the engine reports a payback of 0.0000 years. What does that 0 mean?', '["The project never had any money at risk.", "Payback on discounted cash, positive throughout.", "No crossing was found, so 0 is a refusal.", "The cumulative was non-negative at index 0, where payback is read, and the reading is never revisited after the second 130.0000 takes the project under water."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Payback is read where the cumulative is first non-negative, and OKPOMA''s split capex puts that at index 0. The project was still under water after its second year, which the payback never shows.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-case-before-the-numbers', 8, 'The Probabilistic Breakeven Analyzer''s year 1 tax kink on ISIALA sits at 160.9994 USD per bbl. What does that number reflect that the quick form never builds?', '["The 90th percentile of the breakeven price, the high end of the sampled run.", "The price at which the twenty year NPV crosses zero with the capex split as in the quick case.", "All capex placed in year 1 and expensed there, so that year''s taxable income needs a far higher price to cross zero.", "A development year with no production, the shape of the published 10 year base case, which charges its capex against zero revenue in its first row."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A kink is the price at which one year''s taxable income crosses zero. The quick form splits capex 50/50 over two years, so it never builds a year carrying the whole 180.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-case-before-the-numbers', 9, 'The published 10 year base case opens with capex of 400.0000 against grossRevenue of 0.0000 and has a maxExposure of -400.0000. Why can the quick form not produce it?', '["It can, by setting the initial rate to 0, since the decline then starts later.", "It refuses any capex schedule except 50/50 over the first two years, and oil flows from the first year, so a development year cannot be typed.", "Its capex field is capped below 400, which this case exceeds.", "It can, since the quick form places the whole capex in year 1 whenever the capex exceeds the first year''s gross revenue, which reproduces the opening row of the base case exactly."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The base case was built year by year. The quick form''s first row always produces, as ISIALA''s 1606000.0000 bbl in 2027 shows while 90.0000 of capex is spent.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-case-before-the-numbers', 10, 'The Low scenario raises ISIALA''s total capex to 216.0000 and the High scenario lowers it to 144.0000. How does the case place each figure?', '["Half in each of the first two years, the same 50/50 split as the base case''s 180.", "The change from 180 lands in 2027 as an overrun or a saving on top of the base split, and 2028 keeps its original 90.0000.", "All of it in 2027, since a scenario rebuilds the case with a development year before it scales capex.", "Spread evenly over twenty years, since a scenario scales every row together."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The split refuses any schedule except 50/50 over the first two years, so 216.0000 and 144.0000 are each divided into two equal halves, just as 180 became 90.0000 and 90.0000.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-case-before-the-numbers', 11, 'ISIALA''s OPEX sensitivity bar swings -8.6463 between 0.7 and 1.3. A reader concludes that ISIALA barely cares about operating cost. What did the bar measure?', '["All of ledger opex, and the swing is small because opex is a small share of revenue in every year of the life.", "Variable opex only, since fixed opex is treated as a sunk cost the sweep leaves untouched.", "Fixed and variable opex together, discounted, so the small swing is an honest measure.", "Only fixed opex, 2.5 million USD a year."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The OPEX bar scales opexFixed only. The variable half, 20.8780 in 2027 alone, is never flexed by that bar, so the reader has measured the small half of ISIALA''s opex.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-case-before-the-numbers', 12, 'ISIALA''s Production and Oil Price bars both read -17.3893 at 0.7 and 175.8952 at 1.3. Why are they identical?', '["Revenue is volume times price, so any bar scaling either term must match.", "The two bars share one sweep inside the engine.", "The Production bar scales oil volume only and variable opex does not follow it, so the scaled barrels arrive with no extra cost, exactly as a higher price would.", "Production also scales variable opex."]'::jsonb, 2, 'On a field with variable opex those two bars should differ. Here they do not, because the case holds variable opex as fixed millions per year once it has been built.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-case-before-the-numbers', 13, 'ISIALA''s Low scenario reads totalOpex 220.4887 and the High scenario 200.4887, the same distance either side of the Base 210.4887, although Low produces less oil than High. Why?', '["The scenarios scale fixed opex by a fifth either way and never scale variable opex, so the whole distance is fixed opex.", "Variable opex falls with Low''s production while fixed opex rises by more, and the two effects happen to net to the same distance on ISIALA.", "The scenarios scale total ledger opex by a fifth either way, and the base total is small enough that the two moves come out the same size.", "Opex is sampled in both scenarios with the same seed."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Twenty years of fixed opex at 2.5 is raised or lowered by a fifth, while variable opex keeps the base case''s millions per year although Low produces less oil than High.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-case-before-the-numbers', 14, 'ISIALA is priced at 70 USD per bbl, below the breakeven engine''s base breakeven of 71.6277, yet the screening engine reports an NPV of 81.0464. Which reading is right?', '["The field is uneconomic at its own price, and 81.0464 comes from mid-year discounting overstating a value that year-end discounting would show to be negative.", "The screening NPV is right and the breakeven is wrong, since the breakeven engine charges opex twice once its efficiency is applied to the barrels.", "The breakeven is the median of the sampled run, so a price of 70 sits inside its range and the two engines agree once uncertainty is counted.", "The two engines run different cases, so a price from one does not test the other."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The breakeven engine places all capex in year 1, charges opex of 20 million USD a year flat and applies an efficiency of 0.91, and through it the NPV at 70 is -6.5653. The screening case splits capex over two years and charges 2.5 fixed plus 13 USD per bbl.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-case-before-the-numbers', 15, 'What price paths can the Scenario Builder''s Monte Carlo draw around ISIALA''s flat 70 USD per bbl?', '["One price drawn per iteration and held flat for all twenty rows of the case.", "A price drawn for each year around 70, with no trend, since the case holds no deck and no escalation.", "A deck that escalates from 70 at a sampled rate.", "A random walk, with each year''s price starting from the year before''s draw, so that a run can trend up or down across the life of the field."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The flat price refuses a deck, escalation and inflation. The Monte Carlo can draw a price for each year around 70 and never draws a trend, and 70.0000 in 2046 is the same number of USD as 70.0000 in 2027.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-royalty-and-tax-ledger', 1, 'A spreadsheet books ISIALA''s 2027 grossRevenue as 4400 times 70. What is wrong with that column?', '["Nothing, since 4400 times 70 divided by 1e6 gives the same 112.4200 once the units are matched.", "It leaves in the 15 percent royalty, which the engine takes out before it books the line.", "It forgets the first year''s 12 percent decline, which the engine applies to 4400 bopd before multiplying the annual volume by the price and scaling the result into million USD.", "It prices a day''s volume in USD; the engine prices the annual 1606000.0000 bbl and divides by 1e6 to reach 112.4200 million USD."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A column built from the daily rate is too small by a year of days, and both that error and a missing 1e6 flow straight into royalty and tax. Royalty is its own line, taken after grossRevenue is booked.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-royalty-and-tax-ledger', 2, 'The published tr_missing_profiles case has grossRevenue of 0.0000 in 2032 and 2033. What does the engine charge in those years?', '["Nothing, since a row with no revenue is dropped from the ledger and the life ends when the profile runs out.", "Opex of 5.0000 each year, for a net cash flow of -5.0000.", "Opex of 5.0000 offset by a negative tax, so each net cash flow is smaller in magnitude than the opex.", "The last year''s revenue again, since a profile shorter than the life is extended at its final value."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A profile shorter than the life reads as zero beyond its end, opex is still charged, and tax is 0.0000 because the engine never returns a negative tax. The engine does not notice that the field stopped.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-royalty-and-tax-ledger', 3, 'OKPOMA pays royalty of 15.8749 in 2028, a year whose net cash flow is -11.4786. Why is any royalty due?', '["Royalty is 10 percent of that year''s gross revenue of 158.7487, taken before any cost, whether the year makes money or not.", "It is 2027''s royalty deferred a year, since 2027 had a positive base.", "Royalty is charged on the positive part of taxable income, which was still positive before capex was expensed in 2028 and so attracts the flat rate.", "The engine charges a minimum royalty in any capex year, to keep the government''s take above zero in a year when no tax is due."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Royalty is grossRevenue times royaltyRate divided by 100 and refuses any relief in a loss year. OKPOMA even takes it off the top of 2046''s gross revenue of 4.4603, when opex exceeds that revenue.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-royalty-and-tax-ledger', 4, 'A reader''s ISIALA ledger shows a 2027 govTake of 0.0000. What does that tell you?', '["The ledger is right, since no tax was due in 2027.", "govTake excludes royalty until payback.", "Royalty was taken after costs, as if it were a profit tax; the engine charges 16.8630 off gross revenue in that loss year, and govTake equals it.", "Royalty was deferred to 2029 with the first tax."]'::jsonb, 2, 'In a year with no tax govTake is the royalty alone, so a zero there means royalty was read from the wrong line.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-royalty-and-tax-ledger', 5, 'ISIALA''s royalty rate is 15 percent and its tax rate 35 percent, yet totalRoyalty of 129.6255 sits close to totalTax of 136.0307. Why?', '["Tax is charged on net cash flow, which is roughly a third of revenue, so a 35 percent rate on it lands near the royalty.", "Royalty is charged on a larger base in all twenty years, and tax on a smaller base in 18 of them.", "The totals are discounted, and tax is paid later than royalty, so its discounted total is pulled down toward the royalty.", "Royalty compounds on the year before."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Royalty comes off gross revenue from the first barrel, while tax is 35 percent of a base already reduced by royalty, opex and expensed capex, and it is 0.0000 in 2027 and 2028.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-royalty-and-tax-ledger', 6, 'ISIALA''s Low scenario reads totalRevenue 553.0688 and totalRoyalty 82.9603, both in proportion to the Base, but totalTax of 62.9637, less than half the Base 136.0307. Why does tax fall faster?', '["Low runs at a lower tax rate, since the scenarios scale the tax rate with price.", "Royalty is taken twice in Low, on revenue and on scaled production.", "Low carries its capex year losses forward, so the deductions from the two capex years reduce every later year''s tax until they are used up, which the Base case never needs to do at all.", "Tax depends on costs as well as revenue, and Low raises capex and fixed opex by a fifth while its revenue falls, so the taxable base shrinks faster than revenue."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Royalty is a fixed fraction of the first line and scales one for one with revenue. Tax is charged on what is left after costs, and in Low the costs rose while revenue fell; there is no loss carry forward in either case.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-royalty-and-tax-ledger', 7, 'The published hand case has gross revenue 100, royalty 20, opex 10 and capex 50 in year 1, taxed at 50 percent with immediate expensing. What is year 1''s tax?', '["10, on a base of 20.", "35, since capex is a cash item that never enters the tax base, leaving 70 to be taxed.", "5, half of the year''s net cash flow of 10, since tax is charged on what the contractor keeps.", "20, on a base of 40, since royalty is paid to the government and does not come off the tax base."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Taxable income is 100 minus 20 minus 10 minus 50, which is 20, and half of it is 10. Year 2 has no capex, so its base is 70 and its tax is 35.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-royalty-and-tax-ledger', 8, 'Setting capexDepreciationYears to 2 on the hand case leaves total tax at 45.0000 but lowers the NPV from 39.8721 to 38.7886. Why?', '["Cash capex is spread to 25 a year.", "A deduction is lost past the end of the life.", "Year 1 deducts only 25 of the 50, so tax becomes 22.5 in both years instead of 10 then 35, and more of the same 45.0000 is paid a year sooner.", "Royalty rises with the depreciation schedule."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Cash out is unchanged at 70 and net cash flow becomes -2.5 then 47.5. The NPV falls because tax moved earlier, while the total tax did not change.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-royalty-and-tax-ledger', 9, 'On the two year hand case, capexDepreciationYears of 5 gives total tax of 60.0000 against 45.0000 at 1. Where did the extra tax come from?', '["A longer schedule charges interest on the undepreciated balance, which the engine adds to the tax line in each year of the life.", "Five year depreciation is recaptured as income in year 2.", "The engine raises the tax rate when the schedule runs past the life, as a penalty for a case whose depreciation cannot be completed.", "Deductions scheduled after year 2 fall past the end of the life and are lost, so part of the capex is never deducted at all."]'::jsonb, 3, 'As the schedule lengthens total tax climbs from 45.0000 through 53.3333 and 57.5000 to 60.0000, because the depreciation that would land in years 3 to 5 has no year to land in.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-royalty-and-tax-ledger', 10, 'ISIALA''s 2027 and 2028 taxable income is negative. How much of those losses comes off the 2029 base that produces tax of 19.3660?', '["None; 19.3660 is 35 percent of 87.0580 minus 13.0587 minus 18.6679, that year''s own base.", "All of it, carried forward, so the 2029 tax is less than 35 percent of that year''s own base.", "Half of it, carried one year at a time.", "The 2028 loss only, since each year''s base is reduced by the loss of the year immediately before it and the 2027 loss expires unused."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The tax base refuses a loss carry forward. A reader who carries the losses forward writes a smaller 2029 tax than the engine charges, and a higher NPV than the engine reports.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-royalty-and-tax-ledger', 11, 'OKPOMA pays tax of 6.1666 in 2027, a year in which 130.0000 of capex is spent and expensed. Why is tax due?', '["The engine charges a minimum tax in every producing year, and 6.1666 is that floor applied to OKPOMA.", "That year''s revenue after royalty and opex exceeds the 130.0000 expensed.", "The 2028 base is brought back a year, since the engine taxes a two year capex split as one period.", "Capex is not deductible in the first production year, so the base is revenue after royalty and opex."]'::jsonb, 1, '193.5960 minus 19.3596 minus 28.8200 minus 130.0000 leaves a positive base, and 40 percent of it is 6.1666. The 2028 base is negative and pays 0.0000.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-royalty-and-tax-ledger', 12, 'The hand case with capexDepreciationYears 2 pays tax of 22.5000 in year 1 while that year''s net cash flow is -2.5000. How can a year that loses money pay tax?', '["Tax is 22.5 percent of gross revenue.", "The engine refunds it in year 2.", "Tax follows the tax base, which deducts only 25 of the 50 capex that year while cash pays all 50, so the base is positive and the cash is negative.", "It is year 2''s tax paid early."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Year 1''s base is 100 minus 20 minus 10 minus 25, which is 45, and half of that is 22.5000. A negative net cash flow does not always mean zero tax.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-royalty-and-tax-ledger', 13, 'Why does the one-sided tax rule make the screening NPV bend as the oil price moves, instead of following one straight line?', '["Each year''s tax switches on at its own price, so the slope of NPV against price changes each time another year''s base turns positive.", "Royalty grows faster than revenue at high prices.", "Discounting bends the line, since a higher price moves value into later years.", "Tax refunds on negative bases grow as the price falls, flattening the low end of the line more than the high end, which gives the line its curve."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A negative base pays nothing and remembers nothing, so below a year''s switching price that year''s tax is 0.0000 and above it the year pays the full rate on its base.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-royalty-and-tax-ledger', 14, 'A reader closes ISIALA''s rows by subtracting the depreciation column as well as capex. What goes wrong?', '["Nothing, since depreciation equals capex on ISIALA and the two subtractions cancel.", "Only the tax changes, because depreciation is a tax line and the net cash flow column rebuilds tax from the base before the cumulative is summed.", "The NPV rises, since the larger deduction lowers tax in the capex years.", "Each 90.0000 of 2027 and 2028 is charged twice, so the ledger sits deeper than -44.6035 and pays back later than 3.2746 years."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Net cash flow is gross revenue less royalty, capex, opex and tax. Depreciation only ever changed the tax, so taking it off again counts a cost that was spent once.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-royalty-and-tax-ledger', 15, 'ISIALA''s final cumulative is 208.0250 and its NPV is 81.0464. Which reading is right?', '["208.0250 is the value, and 81.0464 is what remains after tax is deducted a second time at the discount rate.", "81.0464 is wrong, since the NPV of the same rows cannot fall so far below their undiscounted sum.", "Both are correct; 208.0250 is undiscounted.", "208.0250 nets the government''s take of 265.6562 against capex, so it comes from a different ledger than the NPV."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The cumulative says how much money came back and nothing about when: 864.1699 less 129.6255, 180.0000, 210.4887 and 136.0307 leaves 208.0250, and discounting the same rows mid-year at 12 percent gives 81.0464.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-value-from-a-ledger', 1, 'A reviewer rebuilds ISIALA''s twenty net cash flows in a spreadsheet NPV function at 12 percent and gets 76.5817 against the engine''s 81.0464. What should the reviewer conclude?', '["The engine overstates ISIALA, because the spreadsheet function is the standard check and the gap is an engine error that should be corrected down to 76.5817.", "The engine has left 2027 undiscounted with a factor of 1, which treats the first capex year as spent today and lifts the total.", "Both are right on their own conventions: the spreadsheet discounts the first year by a full year, and the ratio of the two is 1.058301, the engine''s first mid-year factor.", "The two apply the rate differently, the engine converting 12 percent to a continuously compounded rate while the spreadsheet compounds it once a year."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine divides year index i by (1.12)^(i + 0.5), so moving every flow half a year earlier scales the whole NPV by (1.12)^0.5, which is 1.058301.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-value-from-a-ledger', 2, 'The published hand case earns net cash flows of 10 and 35 at a 10 percent rate, and the engine prints an NPV of 39.8721. Which expression is it evaluating?', '["10/1.1^0.5 + 35/1.1^1.5", "10/1.1 + 35/1.1^2, the year-end form a spreadsheet uses", "10 + 35/1.1, with the first year left undiscounted at a factor of 1", "10/1.1^1.5 + 35/1.1^2.5, a mid-year shift counted from the end of the first year"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine discounts year index 0 by half a year and index 1 by a year and a half, so the mid-year exponents are 0.5 and 1.5.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-value-from-a-ledger', 3, 'ISIALA''s cumulative net cash flow closes at 208.0250 million USD. Why is that not its value?', '["It becomes the NPV only after one discount at 12 percent over the full twenty years, applied to the closing figure as a single sum.", "It still contains royalty and tax, and the NPV is what remains once the 265.6562 of government take is removed from it.", "It must be discounted row by row, so the NPV is the sum of each year''s cumulative divided by that year''s own factor.", "It is the undiscounted sum; dividing each row''s net cash flow by its mid-year factor and adding gives the NPV of 81.0464."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Discounting the cumulative column counts early cash again in every later year, and quoting 208.0250 as the value overstates ISIALA more than twofold against 81.0464.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-value-from-a-ledger', 4, 'On the 10 year base case the published sweeps give NPVs of 908.4827, 472.6082 and 234.9911 at discount rates of 0, 10 and 20 percent. What happens to its IRR of 41.0683 and payback of 3.1115?', '["Both worsen as the rate rises, since a higher hurdle makes the project recover later and earn less above its cost of capital, so both follow the NPV down the sweep.", "Neither moves: the IRR is the rate that zeroes the NPV and payback reads the undiscounted cumulative, so the discount rate reaches neither.", "The IRR stays fixed but payback lengthens, because the engine recovers the capex from flows discounted at the chosen rate before it reads the crossing year.", "Payback stays fixed but the IRR falls, since the engine measures the IRR as the excess return over the discount rate."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Every discount sweep from 0 to 20 percent reports 41.0683 and 3.1115, and at 0 percent the NPV of 908.4827 equals the closing cumulative.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-value-from-a-ledger', 5, 'OKPOMA''s 2046 row has net cash flow of -0.5576 on gross revenue of 4.4603 against opex of 4.5718. What does the screening engine do with that row when it values the field?', '["Drops it, since a row whose opex exceeds its revenue marks the economic limit and the engine ends the project there, valuing only the years before it.", "Discounts it and subtracts it like any other row, because the engine has no economic limit and produces every year of the fixed life.", "Sets it to zero, because tax is charged only on a positive base and the loss is absorbed in the tax column instead.", "Keeps it in the cumulative column but leaves it out of the NPV, since discounting applies only to rows that earn."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A project that would really be shut in is valued as if it kept producing, so the -0.5576 of 2046 is in OKPOMA''s NPV like every other row.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-value-from-a-ledger', 6, 'ISIALA''s cumulative is -8.6381 after 2029 and 22.8165 after 2030, a year that earns 31.4546. How does the engine read payback?', '["Three years, since the engine counts only the whole years finished before the crossing and drops the fraction of 2030.", "The end of 2030, since the engine reports payback at the close of the year in which the cumulative first stops being negative.", "Two whole years plus the fraction 22.8165 / 31.4546, since the crossing is measured back from the surplus at the end of 2030.", "Three whole years finished plus 8.6381 / 31.4546 of 2030, which is 3.2746 years."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The fraction is the shortfall carried into 2030 over that year''s cash, assuming it arrives evenly, so payback is 3 + 8.6381 / 31.4546 = 3.2746.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-value-from-a-ledger', 7, 'ISIALA''s Low scenario reports a payback of 20.0000 years on its twenty year life. How should that be read?', '["It never pays back: the engine reports the project life when the cumulative never stops being negative.", "It recovers in its final year, 2046, when the last row''s positive net cash flow closes the hole just before the life ends.", "The engine clamps payback at the life as it clamps the IRR at 1000 percent, so the true recovery lies past 2046.", "It recovers at the end of the life, because with no economic limit the engine runs every row until the cumulative reaches zero."]'::jsonb, 0, 'payback_never reports 5.0000 on a five period life for the same reason, and a value equal to the life cannot tell recovery at the very end from no recovery.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-value-from-a-ledger', 8, 'OKPOMA''s cumulative is 9.2498 after 2027 and -2.2287 after 2028, and its payback reads 0.0000. What happened?', '["OKPOMA found no crossing from negative to non-negative, and 0.0000 is the engine''s return for a project that never recovers.", "Payback is computed on discounted flows, and the discounted 2027 surplus outweighs the discounted 2028 shortfall.", "The cumulative was non-negative in the first year, so payback was set at 0, and the engine never looked again when the second capex year took it below zero.", "OKPOMA did recover within 2027, and the 2028 dip is new investment that payback is correctly designed to leave out."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Once the cumulative has crossed, the reading is not revisited, so a field that is still 2.2287 under water at the end of 2028 prints a payback of 0.0000.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-value-from-a-ledger', 9, 'At 40 USD per bbl the 10 year base case pays back at 8.4377 years with an NPV of -123.6336. How can both be true?', '["Payback is struck at a 10 percent rate and the NPV at a higher hurdle, so the two readings use different discount rates and cannot be compared.", "The NPV includes the abandonment cost of 50 in the last year, which payback excludes as a non-operating item that falls after recovery.", "Payback is measured before tax and the NPV after tax, so a lower price hits the NPV harder than it hits recovery.", "Payback uses the undiscounted cumulative, so the project returns its money in nominal terms while its discounted value is negative."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every discount rate from 0 to 20 percent returns the same payback of 3.1115 on this case, and at 40 USD per bbl recovery at 8.4377 years still destroys 123.6336 of value.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-value-from-a-ledger', 10, 'Would ISIALA''s IRR of 53.7148 percent change if the engine discounted at year end instead of mid-year?', '["Yes, it would fall by the ratio 1.058301, the same factor that separates the mid-year NPV of 81.0464 from 76.5817 at year end.", "No: every term carries the same extra (1 + r)^-0.5, so the rate that zeroes the year-end NPV also zeroes the mid-year one.", "Yes, it would rise, since cash that arrives half a year later needs a higher rate to repay the same capex.", "No, because the IRR is computed at the project''s 12 percent discount rate, which the timing convention does not change."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A common factor cannot move a root: the published irr_known_21pct case, -100 then +121, returns 21.0000 under mid-year discounting.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-value-from-a-ledger', 11, 'NTEJE loses 123.9923 million USD, never pays back, and reports an IRR of 1000.0000 percent. What is that IRR?', '["The Newton clamp reported as a rate, FINDINGS S1: NTEJE has no IRR to find, yet a sort by IRR would put it first.", "A true root lying beyond the clamp, the same situation as irr_beyond_clamp, where the engine reports 1000 for a root at 9900 percent.", "A real but extreme rate, because NTEJE''s early revenue is large against its capex and recovers the spend inside its first year.", "The engine''s return for net cash flows that never change sign, printed high so it cannot pass for a rate."]'::jsonb, 0, 'NTEJE''s NPV never reaches zero at any rate, so there is no root at all; irr_beyond_clamp is the different case of a real root at 9900 percent past the clamp. The no sign change return is 0.0000, not 1000.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-value-from-a-ledger', 12, 'Net cash flows of -100, 230 and -132 have roots at 10 and 20 percent. What does the engine report as the IRR?', '["20.0000, the larger root, since the engine searches for the highest rate at which the NPV is zero.", "0.0000, the fixed return the engine uses when the flows change sign more than once and no single IRR exists.", "10.0000, the root Newton lands on from its 10 percent start, with nothing to say a second root exists.", "1000.0000, the clamp, because Newton oscillates between the two roots until the step runs away."]'::jsonb, 2, 'irr_two_roots returns 10.0000 while the golden lists roots at 10 and 20 percent, so the reported rate is one of two with no flag.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-value-from-a-ledger', 13, 'Cash flows of order 1e-7 million USD have a true IRR of 21 percent, and the engine returns 10.0000. Why?', '["The engine rounds any flow below its four decimal print to zero, sees no sign change and reports its default for that case.", "Newton converged on the second of two roots, which for flows this small sits at 10 percent instead of 21.", "Mid-year discounting shifts a 21 percent year-end root down to 10 percent once every flow is small enough.", "An absolute guard on the slope, |dNPV/dr| < 1e-5, fires on such tiny flows and hands back Newton''s 10 percent starting guess."]'::jsonb, 3, 'irr_tiny_cash_flows_derivative_guard is irr_known_21pct scaled down, and the guard fires on the first iteration, so 10.0000 is the start, not a root.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-value-from-a-ledger', 14, 'ISIALA''s High scenario prints a peak exposure, maxExposure, of 27.7707. How is it read?', '["As the lowest cumulative of a project never under water, positive, so it needs no funding.", "As a funding requirement of 27.7707 million USD, the depth of the hole that 2029''s positive cash flow later repays.", "As the discounted trough, positive because mid-year factors shrink the capex rows more than the revenue rows.", "As the gap between High''s capex of 144.0000 and its first year revenue, which a larger revenue keeps above zero."]'::jsonb, 0, 'maxExposure is the minimum of the undiscounted cumulative, so dropping its sign turns a project that never needs cash into one that seems to need 27.7707.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-value-from-a-ledger', 15, 'ISIALA''s peak exposure is -44.6035 against capex of 180, while the 10 year base case''s is the full -400.0000. Why is ISIALA''s hole so shallow?', '["Peak exposure is struck on discounted rows, and ISIALA''s 12 percent rate shrinks its capex years more than the base case''s 10 percent does.", "Peak exposure counts only the first year''s capex, 90.0000, and leaves out the second half that ISIALA spends in 2028.", "The quick form produces from its first year, so ISIALA sells 112.4200 of oil in 2027 against 90.0000 of capex, while the base case has a development year with no revenue.", "ISIALA''s capex is expensed for tax in the year it is spent, and the relief on 180 is added back to the cumulative before the minimum is taken."]'::jsonb, 2, 'ISIALA''s cumulative is -17.8210 after 2027 and -44.6035 after 2028, its deepest point, while the base case spends 400.0000 against gross revenue of 0.0000.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-one-number-becomes-three', 1, 'On ISIALA the Production row of the sensitivity sweep prints -17.3893 and 175.8952, exactly the Oil Price row to four decimals. Why?', '["The sweep treats revenue as a single input, and both labels point at the same revenue multiplier inside the engine.", "Production scales production.oil only, and variable opex is already stored in million USD a year from the base volume, so scaling volume moves revenue exactly as price does while that cost stays put.", "Production and price enter only as a product, and royalty and tax are flat rates on that product, so any field ties the two rows whatever its costs.", "The decline is scaled along with production, so volume lost in early years is offset exactly by volume kept in later years."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Variable opex of 20.8780 in 2027 was worked out at 13 USD per bbl on the base volume and does not follow a scaled volume, and the 10 year base case repeats the tie at 95.1220 to 850.0945.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-one-number-becomes-three', 2, 'How would a real 30 percent volume shortfall on ISIALA compare with the Production row''s printed -17.3893?', '["It would cost exactly the printed amount, since price and volume are interchangeable in revenue and the sweep shows the two rows tied to four decimals.", "It would cost more than printed, because a lower volume raises fixed opex per barrel and the engine omits that unit cost.", "It would cost more than printed, since a shortfall compounds through the decline year on year and the sweep scales year one only.", "It would cost less than printed, because fewer barrels also means less variable opex, which the Production row holds at the base volume."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The row is an engine property read as physics: the sweep leaves variable opex at the base, so the true loss from lower volume is smaller than the gap from 81.0464 down to -17.3893.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-one-number-becomes-three', 3, 'The OPEX row at 0.7 and 1.3 moves ISIALA only from 85.3696 to 76.7233. What does that row actually scale?', '["Fixed opex of 2.5 million USD a year, and nothing else; variable opex, 20.8780 in 2027, is untouched.", "Total opex, which is small beside revenue, so the row shows that opex hardly matters to the value of this field.", "Variable opex only, since the fixed 2.5 a year is treated as a sunk overhead that the sweep leaves at the base.", "Both opex terms together, with tax relief at 35 percent and royalty at 15 percent absorbing most of each change."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Only about a tenth of ISIALA''s first year opex is in the row, so reading it as opex does not matter misreads an engine choice as a field property.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-one-number-becomes-three', 4, 'The sweep reports ISIALA at -17.3893 when price is 0.7 of 70.0000 USD per bbl. What does it say about how likely that is?', '["That it is the 10th percentile of price, since a 30 percent fall is the conventional low end of an oil price belief.", "That it is exactly as likely as the 1.3 end, because the sweep is symmetric about the base and so are its odds.", "Nothing: it attaches no probability to either end and uses the same plus and minus 30 percent for every input.", "That it is the worst outcome the engine expects, since nothing outside 0.7 to 1.3 is ever swept for any input."]'::jsonb, 2, 'No distribution stands behind either end, so calling 0.7 of 70.0000 a 10th percentile misuses a percentile word; -17.3893 is only what happens if price is there.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-one-number-becomes-three', 5, 'ISIALA''s swings, high minus low, are 193.2845 for Oil Price and Production, -93.4835 for CAPEX and -8.6463 for OPEX. A tornado chart sorts on the signed number. What goes wrong?', '["Nothing, because the sign only records direction and sorting on it gives the same order as sorting on width for every bar on the chart.", "CAPEX lands above Oil Price, since a negative swing is read as a loss and a tornado places losses at the top.", "Production drops to the bottom, because a tie on the signed number is broken by each input''s position in the table.", "OPEX lands above CAPEX, since -8.6463 is larger than -93.4835, when by width CAPEX belongs directly below the tied pair."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The sign records direction, raising capex or opex lowers NPV, so bars must be sorted by width: 193.2845, then 93.4835, then 8.6463.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-one-number-becomes-three', 6, 'ISIALA''s Oil Price bar runs from -17.3893 to 175.8952 around a base of 81.0464, and the drop below the base is larger than the rise above it. Why?', '["Mid-year discounting weights the downside more heavily, since lost revenue is assumed to arrive half a year earlier than gained revenue.", "ISIALA pays no tax in 2027 and 2028, so revenue lost there costs in full, while extra revenue pushes 2027 into tax and the state takes a share of the gain.", "The engine carries the downside run''s early losses forward against later tax, so the same drop is repeated in every tax paying year from 2029 through to 2046.", "Royalty comes off the top only when the price rises, so the upside is trimmed by 15 percent while the downside is not."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine taxes only a positive base and carries no loss forward, so the two sides of 81.0464 are taxed differently in the two capex years.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-one-number-becomes-three', 7, 'ISIALA''s CAPEX bar is 126.2382 at 0.7 and 32.7547 at 1.3 around 81.0464. Which end lies further from the base, and why?', '["The 1.3 end: extra capex lands in years that already pay no tax, so it gets no relief, while saved capex creates taxable income that is partly taxed away.", "The 0.7 end: saved capex is untaxed cash in the first two years, where the mid-year factors discount it least.", "Neither: capex is expensed immediately, so every change is relieved at the 35 percent tax rate equally on both sides.", "The 1.3 end: extra capex is spread by depreciation over the life, so its tax relief arrives late and is discounted hard."]'::jsonb, 0, '32.7547 sits further below 81.0464 than 126.2382 sits above it, the mirror of the price asymmetry, because tax is 0.0000 in 2027 and 2028 and the engine carries no loss forward, so 2029''s tax of 19.3660 is charged on that year''s own base.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-one-number-becomes-three', 8, 'Why are the OPEX bar''s two ends, 85.3696 and 76.7233, symmetric about 81.0464 to within a rounding of the last decimal?', '["Opex is not deductible for tax, so a change reaches the NPV undiluted and by the same amount in both directions.", "The engine discounts opex at year end rather than mid-year, which makes its effect on the NPV exactly linear in the multiplier.", "Scaling only the fixed 2.5 million USD a year never flips a year into or out of tax, so both ends are taxed alike.", "The row is run once and mirrored by the chart, since an opex swing of 8.6463 is too small for the engine to run twice."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Price and capex are lopsided because they move 2027 across the tax line; a change of 30 percent on 2.5 a year leaves 2027 and 2028 untaxed on both sides.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-one-number-becomes-three', 9, 'How does generateScenarios build ISIALA''s Low case?', '["Every sensitivity input at its 0.7 end at once, the same multiplier the sweep uses for one input at a time.", "Price and production times 0.8 and total opex times 1.2, so variable opex rises with the fixed part in the same run.", "Price at its 10th percentile and capex at its 90th percentile, drawn from each input''s stated belief by the scenario generator, with every other input held at its median.", "Price and production times 0.8, capex and fixed opex times 1.2, with royalty, tax, the discount rate, the decline and variable opex left at the base."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The scenarios never scale variable opex and draw from no belief at all, so Low is four fixed multipliers giving -72.1531.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-one-number-becomes-three', 10, 'Low''s total revenue is 553.0688 against the base 864.1699, while capex moves only from 180.0000 to 216.0000. Why does revenue move further in proportion?', '["Low production reaches the economic limit earlier, so the last years of the life produce nothing and earn no revenue.", "Two multipliers act on revenue at once, 0.8 on price and 0.8 on volume, while capex takes the single 1.2.", "The decline is steepened in Low as well as the volume cut, so the late years lose more revenue than the early ones.", "Royalty is charged at a higher rate in Low, and revenue is reported after royalty rather than before it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'High''s 1244.4047 carries both 1.2 factors the same way, while capex moves by the single factor to 144.0000.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-one-number-becomes-three', 11, 'ISIALA''s High scenario prints an IRR of 0.0000 and a payback of 0.0000 beside the best NPV of the three, 237.8860. What are those two numbers?', '["Signs that High earns no return above its cost, and that the engine could not find a year in which it recovers.", "A real root at zero, because High''s scaled-down capex is recovered at exactly the rate at which the NPV vanishes.", "Fixed returns for a ledger that never goes negative: its lowest cumulative is 27.7707, so payback is immediate and, with no sign change, there is no IRR to find.", "The Newton clamp''s floor, the counterpart of the 1000 percent ceiling, reported when the rate runs below zero."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Sorted by IRR, High ranks below Base, 0.0000 against 53.7148, which is a statement about the engine''s fixed return and not about High.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-one-number-becomes-three', 12, 'ISIALA''s Low scenario reports a payback of 20.0000 years and an IRR of -3.5758 percent. Which is a flag and which is a value?', '["The payback is the flag, the project life meaning never; the IRR is a real negative root.", "Both are flags, since the engine never reports a negative IRR and uses the project life as its default payback for every case.", "The IRR is the flag, a clamp at the bottom of Newton''s search for a root, while the payback is a real recovery in 2046.", "Both are values: Low recovers in its twentieth year and earns a small negative return on the way there."]'::jsonb, 0, 'With no economic limit Low still produces and charges all twenty years, and its payback of 20.0000 equals the life, while -3.5758 is a root the engine can print.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-one-number-becomes-three', 13, 'ISIALA''s Low scenario is -72.1531, and the Scenario Builder''s Monte Carlo Low case P90 is 48.7439. Why are they so far apart?', '["The Monte Carlo also samples opex, and cheaper opex draws lift most of its NPVs well above the scenario.", "The scenario multiplies by 0.8 while the sample''s plus or minus 20 percent reaches only 0.9 of the base on a typical draw.", "The sample''s lowest NPV, 16.3054, is its P90, so the scenario sits below it only because 1000 iterations is small.", "The scenario forces price, production, capex and fixed opex bad at once, while the sample draws price, capex and reserves independently each year, so a bad draw is usually offset."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The Monte Carlo never samples opex and draws every year separately, and -72.1531 sits below even the lowest of the 1000 sampled NPVs, 16.3054; calling that lowest value P90 is the misuse, since P90 is the Low case at 48.7439.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-one-number-becomes-three', 14, 'A slide labels ISIALA''s scenario NPVs -72.1531, 81.0464 and 237.8860 as P90, P50 and P10. What is wrong with it?', '["The order, because under the exceedance meaning P10 is the low case, so -72.1531 should carry the label P10 and 237.8860 the label P90.", "No probability was ever computed for a scenario, so -72.1531 is not a value ISIALA meets or exceeds nine times in ten.", "Nothing, since Low, Base and High are simply the conventional names for P90, P50 and P10 on any NPV table a screening study prints.", "Only the middle label, since Base is a single run while the outer cases are corners and therefore percentiles."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A label needs a definition behind it: P90 on an NPV means a 90 percent probability of meeting or exceeding it, and swapping P10 onto the low case is the reversed-label mistake, not a fix.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-one-number-becomes-three', 15, 'The Scenario Builder''s results panel once printed 109.8980 under "P90 (Conservative)". What was wrong with that card?', '["Nothing: 109.8980 is the High case P10, and the conservative card was meant to show how much upside the project keeps.", "It showed a scenario rather than a sample, since 109.8980 is the High scenario that generateScenarios returns.", "It held the p90 key, the larger number, when under the exceedance meaning the P90 Low case is 48.7439.", "It showed the mean of the 1000 NPVs, which the panel had mislabelled as a percentile because the two sit close together."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine key p90 is a plain 90th percentile of the sample, so reading it aloud as P90 put the larger of 48.7439 and 109.8980 on the card called conservative.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 1, 'Step one of the capstone method writes ISIALA''s case before any arithmetic. What do 4400 bopd, capex of 180 and variable opex at 13 USD per bbl become in 2027?', '["1606000.0000 bbl, capex of 90.0000 and variable opex of 20.8780 million USD.", "1606000.0000 bbl, the whole capex of 180.0000 in the first year, and variable opex of 20.8780 million USD.", "1413280.0000 bbl declined once, capex 90.0000, variable opex 18.3726.", "1606000.0000 bbl, capex of 90.0000 and variable opex of 23.3780 million USD, the rate applied to the year''s volume."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The quick form splits capex half in each of the first two years and prices variable opex as volume times 13 over a million, and 23.3780 is total opex with the fixed 2.5 included.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 2, 'Proving ISIALA''s 2027 row by hand, why is its tax 0.0000?', '["Tax is deferred to 2029, when the capex is recovered, and charged then.", "The quick form grants a tax holiday in both capex years.", "Revenue less royalty, capex and opex is negative in 2027, and the engine charges tax only on a positive base.", "Royalty of 16.8630 is credited against tax and exceeds the liability."]'::jsonb, 2, '112.4200 less royalty 16.8630, capex 90.0000 and opex 23.3780 is negative, so tax is 0.0000 and the net cash flow is -17.8210, with no loss carried forward.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 3, 'Building ISIALA''s 2029 row by hand, a learner gets a tax higher than 19.3660. Which slip does the method''s row check point to?', '["Capex of 90.0000 was expensed in 2029 rather than in 2028, when the second half was actually spent.", "Tax was charged on the net cash flow of 35.9654.", "The 2029 base was divided by its mid-year factor of 1.327532 before the 35 percent rate was taken.", "Royalty was left in the tax base instead of being taken off the top."]'::jsonb, 3, 'In 2029 the base is 87.0580 less royalty 13.0587 and opex 18.6679, and 35 percent of it is 19.3660; only a base that still carries royalty comes out higher.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 4, 'Reading ISIALA''s value metrics against their flags, which engine values should send a reader back to the net cash flow column?', '["A negative NPV, an IRR below the 12 percent discount rate, and any payback beyond ISIALA''s 3.2746 years.", "An IRR of 0, 10 or 1000, a payback equal to the life, and a payback of 0.", "Any IRR above ISIALA''s own 53.7148 percent, since Newton''s clamp is what stops a sound screening case reporting a higher rate.", "A negative peak exposure, since the engine prints the trough as a positive size whenever a project is sound."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Those values are fixed returns that print like readings: a payback of 20.0000 means never, and ISIALA''s 53.7148 is trusted only after the net cash flow is seen to change sign.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 5, 'Writing OKPOMA''s case before any arithmetic, as step one of the capstone method asks, a candidate reaches 2046, where opex of 4.5718 already exceeds gross revenue of 4.4603. What must the case still hold for that year?', '["There is no economic limit: every year of the 20 year life is produced and charged, even one that loses money.", "OKPOMA reached its economic limit in 2046, and the engine prints the row only to mark the year production stopped.", "The quick form books OKPOMA''s abandonment in its final year, and that closing charge inside the 4.5718 of opex is what pushes 2046 below zero.", "Tax has turned negative, refunding 40 percent of the loss to the operator."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine always builds a 20 year case and charges 4.5718 of opex against 4.4603 of revenue in 2046, so that loss sits inside the value like any other row; the quick form books no abandonment cost at all.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 6, 'The method''s first closing check subtracts royalty 129.6255, capex 180.0000, opex 210.4887 and tax 136.0307 from ISIALA''s revenue of 864.1699. What must be left?', '["81.0464, the NPV, since the totals on the summary card are the discounted sums of the rows.", "265.6562, the government take, since royalty and tax are what the subtraction isolates.", "208.0250, the closing cumulative net cash flow.", "44.6035, the peak exposure, which the totals reproduce once all the capex has been repaid."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The totals are undiscounted, so they close on the cumulative column, 208.0250, and never on the NPV of 81.0464.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 7, 'The method''s last closing check divides ISIALA''s mid-year NPV of 81.0464 by its year-end NPV of 76.5817. What must it give, and why?', '["1.12, the full annual factor, because the year-end NPV is the mid-year NPV discounted by one more whole year.", "1.058301, the first mid-year factor, because moving every flow half a year earlier scales every discounted value by (1.12)^0.5.", "1.058301, but only because of ISIALA''s own rows, since a different decline or capex split would change the ratio.", "1.185297, the 2028 factor, because the two capex years set the gap between the two discounting conventions."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The ratio is fixed by the rate alone: every term shifts by the same half year, so any ledger at 12 percent gives 1.058301.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 8, 'Why does the capstone method refuse to read ISIALA''s metrics straight off the summary card?', '["The card rounds to two decimals, so a payback of 3.2746 read from it lands in the wrong year.", "The card discounts at year end, so it disagrees with the ledger by 1.058301.", "The card shows the High scenario, whose IRR of 0.0000 is not the base case.", "Every number there is true to the engine, yet payback and IRR can be fixed returns."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Nothing on the card is wrong to the engine; the danger is a payback of 20.0000 or an IRR of 0.0000 that print like readings, so each is checked against its flag.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 9, 'Which limit does the capstone method state about its own checks on ISIALA?', '["It proves two rows and trusts eighteen, and it cannot say whether the price or the decline is right.", "It proves every one of the twenty rows, so a case that passes its checks is right in price and decline too.", "It checks the value metrics and the range but never proves a ledger row.", "It attaches a probability to Low and High from the rows it has checked."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The method proves the 2027 and 2029 rows, -17.8210 and 35.9654, and attaches no probability to the range from -72.1531 to 237.8860.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 10, 'In ISIALA''s 2029 row, what is the government take and how is it built?', '["19.3660, the tax alone, since royalty is a cost the operator pays rather than part of the take.", "13.0587, the royalty alone, since tax on a screening ledger is corporate and sits outside government take.", "14.8394, since take is charged on the previous year''s income and so lags the row it is printed on.", "32.4247, royalty of 13.0587 plus tax of 19.3660."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Government take on every row is royalty plus tax, and across the life it closes at 265.6562, royalty 129.6255 plus tax 136.0307.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 11, 'ISIALA''s capex belief is a 10th percentile of 150, a median of 180 and a 90th percentile of 220. What goes wrong if those three are used as minimum, mode and maximum?', '["Nothing, because a triangular passes through its minimum, mode and maximum, and so honours all three numbers exactly as stated.", "The distribution''s own 10th percentile, median and 90th percentile come out at 164.4914, 182.5834 and 203.2668: the tails vanish and the range looks narrower than believed.", "The range widens to 127.2260 to 252.3607 and overstates the belief, since both ends are pushed out past the stated points.", "The median moves to 168.6738, the fitted mode, while the two tails stay exactly where the belief put them."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A proper fit gives minimum 127.2260, mode 168.6738 and maximum 252.3607, which passes back through 150, 180 and 220; the ends of a triangle are not its 10th and 90th percentiles.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 12, 'Sampled over 5000 iterations at seed 20260829, ISIALA''s breakeven prices are 62.1713, 73.3297 and 85.5912. How are they named?', '["P90, P50 and P10 under the exceedance meaning, the labels any sampled quantity takes, with 62.1713 read as the P90 low case of the run.", "P10, P50 and P90 in rising order, since the engine keys p10, p50 and p90 already name the three values.", "Low, Best and High cases, since three sampled values of a price take the same case names an NPV does.", "10th percentile, median and 90th percentile of breakeven price, never P-labels, because a higher breakeven is worse."]'::jsonb, 3, 'P-labels and the case names Low, Best and High belong to outcomes where more is better; writing P90 on 62.1713 or reading the key p10 aloud as a P-label is exactly the misuse, and the base breakeven at the medians is 71.6277.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 13, 'ISIALA''s narrow opex belief, 16 / 17 / 26, has a shape ratio of 0.100000. What does the engine do with it?', '["It fits a triangular with minimum 16, mode 17 and maximum 26, which honours the belief exactly as stated.", "It rejects the belief and refuses to run until the median moves.", "No triangular reaches that shape, so the fit is clamped and the engine says so.", "It fits an exact triangular whose tails extend past 16 and 26, as it does for the capex belief of 150 / 180 / 220."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The shape ratio must sit strictly inside 0.381966 to 0.618034, and 0.100000 does not, so the fit uses the most left-skewed triangular there is and carries a note.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 14, 'Through the Scenario Builder''s Monte Carlo on ISIALA, which NPV takes the label P90?', '["109.8980, the High case stored under the engine key p90, which the old panel printed as conservative.", "48.7439, the Low case, stored under the engine key p10.", "81.1835, the Best case, since P90 names the value nearest the outcome expected nine times in ten.", "-72.1531, the Low scenario, since P90 marks the low end of the range and the scenario is its lowest corner."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under the exceedance meaning P90 is the value met or exceeded with 90 percent probability, the Low case 48.7439; the panel that shipped before the fix printed 109.8980 under "P90 (Conservative)".'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 15, 'The Associate reading ends with ISIALA worth -72.1531 to 237.8860 million USD. What can this tier not tell you about that range?', '["How likely any of it is: the sweep and the scenarios say what happens if inputs move, never how often.", "Whether ISIALA has value at the base, since 81.0464 is itself a sampled median.", "Where the ends come from, since the scenario multipliers are drawn at random.", "Anything beyond even odds, since Low and High are mirror multipliers and so are built to be equally likely."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The sweep''s -17.3893 at 0.7 of price and the Low scenario''s -72.1531 are what-if runs with no probability, and the base 81.0464 is one deterministic run.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 1, 'ISIALA''s screening NPV is 81.0464 million USD. A manager reads it as what the project will probably earn. What is it?', '["The Best case P50, because a screening engine runs each input at its median and the median NPV follows from the median inputs.", "The emv, because mid-year discounting averages each year''s cash through the year and so returns the expected value of the ledger.", "The value at the inputs that were typed; ISIALA''s emv of 80.1707 and Best case P50 of 81.1835 happen to land close, and nothing in the engine promises that on the next field.", "A Low case P90, because a deterministic case is built from conservative inputs that the outcome meets or exceeds nine times in ten."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The quick inputs carry no range, so 81.0464 carries no probability; the sampled run over 1000 iterations at seed 20260829 is what supplies the emv of 80.1707.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 2, 'An analyst''s spreadsheet values ISIALA''s ledger rows at 76.5817 million USD, and the screening engine prints 81.0464 for those same rows. Which single difference accounts for it?', '["Only the discounting convention: the spreadsheet discounts the first year by a full year, and moving every flow half a year earlier scales the whole NPV by 1.058301.", "An engine overstatement, which should be corrected down to 76.5817 before the case is set beside Petroleum Economics Studio.", "A ledger difference, since the spreadsheet drops the royalty paid in the two capex years and the gap is that royalty discounted.", "The economic limit, since the spreadsheet stops at the last year that pays and the engine keeps all twenty rows in its sum."]'::jsonb, 0, '81.0464 over 76.5817 is 1.058301, the square root of 1.12 and exactly ISIALA''s 2027 factor; ISIALA''s last year still pays 2.6534, so no limit is involved.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 3, 'OKPOMA''s 2046 row has gross revenue of 4.4603, opex of 4.5718 and a net cash flow of -0.5576. What does its NPV of 167.4389 million USD include?', '["Only the years to 2045, because the engine stops a field once opex first exceeds gross revenue and prints the last row for information only.", "All twenty years with 2046 at zero, because tax cannot go negative and the engine lets the tax line absorb a small operating loss.", "The years worth producing, with 2046 replaced by the abandonment cost that the quick form books in the final year of its life.", "All twenty years, 2046 among them, because the engine has no economic limit."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The -0.5576 is discounted and subtracted like any other row; a real operator would shut the field in, and the quick form books no abandonment cost at all.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 4, 'The Probabilistic Breakeven Analyzer charges ISIALA opex of 20 million USD a year. A reader types 20 into the screening case''s fixed opex field. What has happened?', '["Nothing of substance, since 20 is the total that the screening case''s fixed and variable halves add up to in its first year of production.", "ISIALA is now a different field, carrying 20 fixed plus its 13 USD per bbl variable opex, because the breakeven engine''s 20 is flat total opex; neither engine complains.", "The quick form reads 20 as USD per bbl on entry and adds it to the variable rate, so the variable half more than doubles while fixed opex stays at 2.5.", "The quick form refuses the case, since a fixed opex above the first year''s variable opex of 20.8780 is rejected until it is corrected."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The screening case''s 2027 opex is 23.3780, 2.5000 fixed plus 20.8780 variable, and the breakeven engine charges 20 flat; a figure carried across engines changes the field silently.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 5, 'Which of these reached ISIALA''s case without ever being asked for on the quick form?', '["royaltyRate 15, taxRate 35 and discountRate 12, which the engine writes into the case under its own names and a reviewer checks against the form.", "A 20 year life, TaxRoyalty, gas volume 0 at a gas price of 3.5, capex split 50/50 over the first two years, and no abandonment spend.", "A reserves volume derived from 4400 bopd and the decline, which the engine then uses to decide how many years the case should run.", "The first year of 2027 and an oil price of 70 USD per bbl, both of which the engine fills in whenever the form is left blank."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The five decisions sit in the arrays, so capex of 180 becomes 90.0000 in 2027 and 90.0000 in 2028 whether or not a reviewer opens them; the passthrough rates were typed.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 6, 'A reader applies ISIALA''s 12 percent decline before booking the first year. What do they write for 2027, and what follows?', '["1606000.0000 bbl, the same as the engine, because the quick form rewrites year 1 as the undeclined daily rate over a year whatever the reader does.", "1243686.4000 bbl, because a decline taken before the first year compounds twice by the end of 2027 under the engine''s year on year rule.", "1606000.0000 bbl less a fixed tenth of barrels, which the reader subtracts again every year until the profile runs dry inside the life.", "1413280.0000 bbl, the engine''s 2028, so every row slides a year early and revenue falls in every column."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine books 4400 bopd over a whole year as 1606000.0000 bbl with no decline taken, and the first ratio of 0.880000 appears in 2028; the NPV then falls for a reason unrelated to the field.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 7, 'ISIALA spends 180 million USD of capex yet its maxExposure is only -44.6035, while the 10 year base case prints -400.0000. Why the difference?', '["The quick form produces from its first year, so 112.4200 and 98.9296 of revenue arrive in the same rows as the two 90.0000 capex payments; the base case spends 400.0000 in a year with no revenue.", "maxExposure is struck on discounted rows, so the factor of 1.058301 shrinks ISIALA''s capex while the base case''s development year is left undiscounted.", "ISIALA''s two negative capex year bases earn a tax refund of 35 percent, which a development year with no revenue has no tax base to claim.", "maxExposure counts only the first year''s capex of 90.0000 less its royalty, while the base case counts both years of its capex together."]'::jsonb, 0, 'ISIALA''s cumulative runs -17.8210 then -44.6035 and is positive by the end of 2030; the quick form cannot express the base case''s development year shape.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 8, 'ISIALA''s OPEX bar runs from 85.3696 to 76.7233, a swing of -8.6463. What has that bar actually scaled?', '["All of ISIALA''s opex, fixed and variable together, which shows the field barely cares about the cost of operating it.", "Only the variable 13 USD per bbl, since the sweep treats the fixed annual charge as capex and flexes it on the CAPEX bar instead.", "Only the fixed 2.5 million USD a year, by 0.7 and 1.3, leaving variable opex untouched.", "Both halves by 0.7 and 1.3, with 35 percent of every change absorbed by tax so that the swing shrinks to a small fraction of totalOpex."]'::jsonb, 2, 'runSensitivityAnalysis scales opexFixed ONLY; the variable half, 20.8780 in 2027 alone, is never flexed, so a reader who concludes opex does not matter has measured the wrong half.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 9, 'A tornado for ISIALA draws two bars of exactly equal width, one labelled Production and one Oil Price. What in the expanded case makes them tie?', '["Every cost in the ledger is a fixed share of revenue, so any lever that moves revenue by the same factor must move the NPV by the same amount.", "The engine models variable opex following the barrels, and a price cut shrinks margin per barrel by exactly what fewer barrels shrink it.", "The tornado display merges two bars that differ only past the fourth decimal, so the tie is a rounding of two slightly different sensitivities to revenue.", "Variable opex is stored as millions a year from the base volume, so scaling production moves revenue exactly as price does and the cost stays put."]'::jsonb, 3, 'In a real field fewer barrels means less variable opex, so the true loss from a volume shortfall is smaller than the -17.3893 printed; the tie counts one lever twice.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 10, 'ISIALA''s oil price is 70 USD per bbl and the breakeven engine''s base breakeven at the stated medians is 71.6277. Does that contradict the screening NPV of 81.0464?', '["Yes, since a price below breakeven must give a negative NPV in any engine, and the screening engine''s 81.0464 is lifted by its mid-year convention.", "No, because 71.6277 is the 90th percentile of breakeven price from the sample, and 70 sits comfortably under the sampled median of 73.3297.", "No: the breakeven engine puts all capex in year 1, charges 20 flat opex and applies an efficiency of 0.91, and its own NPV at 70 is -6.5653.", "Yes in part, because both engines run one ledger and the small gap between the two prices is a tax kink that the screening engine skips over."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Both engines are right about their own cases; the screening case splits capex over two years and charges 2.5 fixed plus 13 USD per bbl, so a price from one case does not test the other.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 11, 'Of ISIALA''s lifetime government take of 265.6562 million USD, 129.6255 is royalty charged at 15 percent and 136.0307 is tax charged at 35 percent. How does the lower rate raise almost as much?', '["Royalty is charged on gross revenue in all twenty years; tax falls on a much smaller base after royalty, opex and capex, and in only 18 of them.", "Tax is due only after payback at 3.2746 years, so the first three years are exempt and the lifetime tax falls toward the royalty total.", "The engine carries the 2027 and 2028 losses forward, which strips the deduction for 180 of capex out of later tax and pulls the total down.", "Royalty is applied to net cash flow plus tax, a base close to the tax base, so the two rates of 15 and 35 percent end up acting on almost the same number every year."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Royalty takes 16.8630 in 2027, a loss year with tax of 0.0000; 2029 pays tax of 19.3660 before payback, and the two lines together make govTake of 265.6562.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 12, 'On ISIALA''s Oil Price bar, -17.3893 lies further below 81.0464 than 175.8952 rises above it. Which ledger rule explains the lopsidedness?', '["Mid-year discounting, which weights early years by 1.058301 and later ones by less, so any change is felt harder on the downside than on the upside.", "Tax only on a positive base with no loss carried forward: revenue lost in the untaxed 2027 and 2028 costs in full, while extra revenue there pushes 2027 into tax.", "Royalty off the top in loss years, since a lower price raises royalty''s share of revenue in 2027 and 2028 and deepens the fall below the spine.", "A flat price across twenty rows, since scaling one price by 0.7 compounds downward through the decline faster than scaling it by 1.3 compounds up."]'::jsonb, 1, 'OPEX never flips a year into or out of tax, and its sides of 85.3696 and 76.7233 match either side of 81.0464; a reader who mirrors one side of the price bar overstates the upside.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 13, 'A reviewer assumes a negative net cash flow always means zero tax. Spreading the hand case''s capex deduction over two years gives year 1 a tax of 22.5000 beside cash of -2.5000. What breaks the assumption?', '["The tax base deducts only 25 of year 1''s 50 of capex, while net cash flow subtracts the whole 50 in cash.", "The engine charges a minimum tax on gross revenue whenever a capex schedule is spread over more than one year of the life.", "Year 2''s positive base is carried back into year 1, since a two year schedule links the bases of the two years it spreads across.", "On a spread schedule year 1 is taxed before royalty, so the base becomes 100 less opex and depreciation and the tax rises."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Year 1''s base is 100 minus 20 minus 10 minus 25; total tax stays 45.0000 and the NPV falls from 39.8721 to 38.7886 because more tax is paid sooner.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 14, 'Stretching the hand case''s depreciation schedule from one year to five leaves its cash capex of 50 untouched, yet the NPV drops from 39.8721 to 25.1367 as lifetime tax climbs. What produces the extra tax?', '["Spreading moves deductions into later years with higher revenue, so the capex shelters less tax once discounted at 10 percent.", "A schedule longer than the life is rounded down to 1 year, and the rounding charges tax on the capex as if it had never been spent.", "Deductions scheduled after year 2 are never taken, because the schedule runs past the end of the life and nothing carries them.", "Once a schedule exceeds the life the engine depreciates opex as well, which enlarges the taxable base in both years of the case."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Revenue is a flat 100 in both years, so only the lost deductions move tax: 45.0000 at 1 year, 53.3333 at 3 and 60.0000 at 5, while a value of 0 is floored at 1 and gives 39.8721.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 15, 'A hand-built ISIALA ledger takes 90.0000 of depreciation out of net cash flow in each capex year, on top of the 90.0000 of capex. Which value readings does that misstate, and how?', '["Nothing changes, because the engine itself subtracts depreciation from net cash flow in those years and capex only appears on the tax line.", "Each 90.0000 is charged twice, so the trough sits below -44.6035 and payback lands after 3.2746 years for a cost spent once.", "Only the tax line changes, since the second deduction lowers taxable income in the capex years and so reduces the tax that would be charged there.", "The NPV rises, since the larger deduction in the capex years becomes a loss that reduces the 2029 tax of 19.3660 once it is carried forward."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Depreciation only ever changes the tax; net cash flow is gross revenue less royalty, capex, opex and tax, so 2027 closes at -17.8210 with capex taken once.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 16, 'ISIALA''s closing cumulative net cash flow is 208.0250 and its NPV is 81.0464. How do the two relate?', '["208.0250 is the value before tax and 81.0464 the value after it, the gap being 136.0307 of tax once discounted.", "208.0250 is the NPV at year end and 81.0464 the NPV mid-year, the gap being the half-year shift applied to each of twenty rows.", "208.0250 is the contractor''s cash and 81.0464 the government''s discounted share of it, since govTake is taken from the same column.", "The first is the undiscounted sum of the net cash flows, the second the same flows discounted mid-year at 12 percent."]'::jsonb, 3, 'At a rate of 0 every factor is 1, which is why sweep_discount_0 returns the base case''s closing cumulative of 908.4827; ISIALA at year end is 76.5817, and quoting 208.0250 as value overstates it more than twofold.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 17, 'The 10 year base case returns NPV 908.4827 at a discount rate of 0 and 234.9911 at 20, with IRR 41.0683 and payback 3.1115 at both. What should a reader conclude?', '["Two different fields, since a project worth 908.4827 at one rate and 234.9911 at another cannot share a ledger with one payback.", "The IRR is pinned because Newton starts from the case''s discount rate and converges to the same root from any starting guess.", "One project at two rates; IRR and payback never use the discount rate, so neither can move.", "Payback is discounted at the case rate too, but the crossing year is unchanged and the fraction shifts by less than four decimals show."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Newton starts from 10 percent whatever the case rate, and payback reads the undiscounted cumulative; 472.6082 at 10 percent is the same project again.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 18, 'Which reading of ISIALA''s cumulative column reproduces the engine''s payback of 3.2746 years?', '["2 + 44.6035 / 35.9654: two years finished, then the trough at the end of 2028 over the first positive net cash flow.", "3 + 8.6381 / 31.4546: three whole years finished, then the shortfall carried into 2030 over that year''s net cash flow.", "3 + 8.6381 / 22.8165: three years finished, then the shortfall carried in over the cumulative that 2030 closes at.", "The crossing of the discounted cumulative, read with the same mid-year factors of 1.058301 onward that build the NPV of 81.0464."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The cumulative first stops being negative in index 3, 2030; payback uses no discount rate, and the fraction assumes that year''s 31.4546 arrives evenly through it.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 19, 'OKPOMA''s capex of 260 is split 130.0000 in 2027 and 130.0000 in 2028, and its summary card prints a payback of 0.0000 years beside a maxExposure of -2.2287. How should that payback be read?', '["The cumulative was non-negative at index 0, so payback reads 0, and the engine never revisits it after the second capex year pushes the cumulative under water.", "OKPOMA never had money at risk, since its 2027 gross revenue of 193.5960 covers both halves of the 260 of capex before 2028 begins.", "The engine reads payback from discounted rows, and 2028''s -11.4786 discounted at 10 percent no longer takes the cumulative below zero.", "A payback of 0 flags net cash flows that never change sign, the same fixed return the IRR solver gives a ledger with no negative year."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The cumulative is 9.2498 after 2027 and -2.2287 after 2028: 2027''s gross revenue of 193.5960 pays royalty, opex, tax and only that year''s 130.0000 of capex, never the whole 260, and the second 130.0000 lands after a positive year. maxExposure prints -2.2287 with no year beside it, so only the cumulative column shows the dip.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 20, 'ISIALA''s Low scenario reports a payback of 20.0000 years. What does that number say?', '["Low pays back in its final year, 2046, so it is slow but recovers its 216.0000 of capex.", "Never: a payback equal to the project life is the engine''s way of saying the cumulative never recovers.", "Low pays back after twenty years discounted at 12 percent, which the scenario reports in place of the undiscounted figure.", "Low recovers within the life, and 20.0000 is the fixed life written over the true crossing because the quick form caps payback."]'::jsonb, 1, 'payback_never reports 5.0000 on a five period life the same way, and a value equal to the life cannot tell a recovery at the very end from none; Low''s NPV is -72.1531.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 21, 'Sorted by IRR, a screening shortlist puts NTEJE first at 1000.0000 percent, above ISIALA''s 53.7148, although NTEJE''s NPV is -123.9923 and its payback equals its twenty year life. What is NTEJE''s rate?', '["A root beyond the clamp, like irr_beyond_clamp, whose true rate of 9900 percent the engine can only print as 1000.", "A real return on a small early outlay, the kind of rate a project earns when its first year revenue nearly covers the first half of its capex.", "The Newton clamp reported as a rate: NTEJE''s NPV never changes sign, so there is no root to find at any rate (FINDINGS S1).", "The slope guard''s fixed return, which the engine hands back when a ledger''s cash flows are of order 1e-7 million USD."]'::jsonb, 2, 'NTEJE''s cumulative closes at -154.5906 and payback reads 20.0000. irr_beyond_clamp is the different case of a real root at 9900 percent past the clamp; NTEJE has no root at all, so treat 0, 10 and 1000 as flags until the net cash flow column agrees.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 22, 'ISIALA''s High scenario reports an IRR of 0.0000 percent beside an NPV of 237.8860 and a maxExposure of 27.7707. How should the IRR be read?', '["As a genuine ranking: a larger project earns a lower return per unit of money at risk, so High truly sits below Base at 53.7148.", "As a clamp wrapped round to zero, since Newton ran away past 1000 percent on a project that earns more than it spends in 2027.", "As a real root at zero, since High recovers its 144.0000 of capex by the end of its life and earns no return on it.", "As the engine''s fixed return for no root: High''s net cash flow never changes sign and its cumulative never falls below zero."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Low''s -3.5758 is a real negative root, and High''s payback of 0.0000 is the same kind of flag; read NPV and peak exposure first.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 23, 'The published ledger -100, 230, -132 returns an IRR of 10.0000. What is that return?', '["One of two true roots, 10 and 20 percent; Newton started at 10 and stopped there, and the return does not mention the other.", "The only root, because mid-year discounting removes the second root that the same flows would show under year end discounting.", "The slope guard''s starting guess handed back unchanged, which means Newton found no root at all on flows that change sign twice.", "The case''s discount rate, which the engine returns in place of a rate whenever cash flows change sign more than once."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Every mid-year term carries the same (1 + r)^-0.5, so the year-end roots of 10 and 20 percent survive; the engine reports one root when there are two.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 24, 'The published two year hand case has capex of 50 in year 1, net cash flows of 10 then 35, an NPV of 39.8721 and an IRR of 0.0000. Why zero?', '["The project earns back its 50 of capex exactly by the end of year 1, which is a zero rate of return on the money invested.", "Expensing the 50 in the year it is spent cancels the investment inside the rate calculation, so no outlay is left to earn a return on.", "Year 1''s tax of 10 exactly offsets its net cash flow of 10, and the solver treats a year that nets to zero as the start of the project.", "Year 1 is already positive after capex, so the flows never change sign and the engine returns its fixed 0."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Capex of 50 sits inside year 1''s net cash flow of 10 beside royalty 20, opex 10 and tax 10; payback reads 0.0000 for the same reason, and neither is a statement about value.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 25, 'A summary writes ISIALA''s High scenario as "exposure 27.7707". What has the summary done?', '["Nothing wrong, since peak exposure is reported as a magnitude and the sign on the engine''s value is a display convention only.", "Understated the need, since 27.7707 is the discounted trough and the undiscounted hole is deeper by the first mid-year factor.", "Dropped the sign, so the lowest cumulative of a project never under water reads as a funding need.", "Quoted the funding needed for 2027, the year High spends the first half of its 144.0000 of capex against a smaller revenue."]'::jsonb, 2, 'ISIALA''s Low scenario at -146.2765 and NTEJE at -169.0209 are real holes; maxExposure is plain undiscounted cash and its sign carries the meaning.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 26, 'Which inputs does runSensitivityAnalysis never scale on ISIALA, and what else does it refuse?', '["Capex, since a development cost is treated as sunk in a screening engine and so is left out of every sensitivity row.", "Royalty, tax, the discount rate and the decline; it also never moves two inputs together or puts a probability on either end.", "Production, since the quick form has no reserves field and the volume is fixed by the decline, leaving price to stand in for it.", "None of them, since every quick input is scaled by 0.7 and 1.3 in turn and the table keeps only the four widest rows."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The four rows are Oil Price, CAPEX, OPEX and Production, each at 0.7 and 1.3; -17.3893 is what happens if price is 0.7 of the base, with no view on how likely that is.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 27, 'A tornado sorts ISIALA''s bars on the signed swing, high minus low. Where does that put CAPEX and OPEX?', '["In the same order as a sort by width, because a sign records direction only and cannot change where a bar is ranked.", "CAPEX at the top, because -93.4835 is the largest swing in magnitude once the tie at 193.2845 between the top two is broken.", "OPEX off the chart, because a bar whose two ends match to within a rounding has no width to sort and is dropped from the list.", "OPEX at -8.6463 above CAPEX at -93.4835, the wrong order."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Sorted by width Oil Price and Production tie at 193.2845, CAPEX follows at 93.4835 and OPEX is last; raising capex or opex lowers NPV, which is all the negative sign records.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 28, 'Set ISIALA''s capex at 1.3 times the base and the NPV falls to 32.7547; at 0.7 times it rises to 126.2382. Reading the 2027 and 2028 ledger rows, which end sits further from 81.0464, and what makes it so?', '["The 1.3 end: extra capex lands in 2027 and 2028, years that already pay no tax, so it hurts in full, while saved capex creates taxable income that is partly taxed away.", "The 0.7 end, because saved capex falls in the first two years where the mid-year factor is smallest, so the saving is worth more than the overrun costs.", "Neither end, because capex enters the ledger linearly and is expensed in the year it is spent, which makes the two sides exact mirror images.", "The 1.3 end, because the extra capex becomes a loss carried forward that shelters tax in 2029, where a larger factor makes the relief worth less."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Tax is 0.0000 in 2027 and 2028 and the engine carries no loss forward, so 2029''s tax of 19.3660 is 35 percent of that year''s own base; raising capex takes ISIALA down to 32.7547, further than cutting it lifts it to 126.2382, while OPEX, which never flips a year into or out of tax, stays symmetric.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 29, 'ISIALA''s Low scenario produces less oil than Base yet its totalOpex is 220.4887 against Base''s 210.4887. Why higher?', '["Low''s lower oil price raises the variable cost per barrel, which the engine indexes to revenue rather than to the volume produced.", "Low''s capex of 216.0000 is charged partly through the opex column in the two capex years, which lifts the lifetime opex total.", "Variable opex does follow Low''s 0.8 production downward, but fixed opex at 1.2 rises faster and outweighs the barrels saved.", "Low scales fixed opex by 1.2 and leaves variable opex at the base volume, so the extra is all fixed opex."]'::jsonb, 3, 'High reads 200.4887, the same distance below Base; the scenarios never scale variable opex, which holds at the base volume even while Low produces less.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 30, 'ISIALA''s Low scenario pays totalTax of 62.9637 against Base''s 136.0307, less than half, while totalRoyalty falls only to 82.9603 from 129.6255. Why does tax fall so much further?', '["Low scales the tax rate by 0.8 along with price and production, so each year''s tax falls by two multipliers where royalty falls by one.", "Tax is charged on the residual after royalty, opex and capex, which swings far harder than revenue; royalty is a fixed fraction of revenue.", "A payback of 20.0000 exempts every one of Low''s years from tax, and the 62.9637 is tax the engine charges on the High side of the table.", "Low''s net cash flow is negative over the life, so the engine rebates part of the tax it would have charged the Base case on the same twenty rows of the ledger."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Royalty follows revenue one for one, 553.0688 against 864.1699; the royalty rate, the tax rate and the discount rate are never scaled by the scenarios.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 31, 'A board pack heads the Low, Base and High columns from generateScenarios with P90, P50 and P10, so ISIALA''s Low column now reads as its P90. What is wrong?', '["Only the order, since under the exceedance meaning the low value -72.1531 should carry P10 and the high value 237.8860 should carry P90.", "Nothing, since the multipliers of 0.8 and 1.2 are chosen as the 10th and 90th percentile of each input and the NPVs inherit those odds.", "It gives -72.1531 odds it never had: no probability was computed for any scenario, and P90 on an NPV is a value met or exceeded with 90 percent probability.", "Only the middle label, since the Best case P50 of 81.1835 belongs there, and the two outer scenarios are then the tails of the same sample."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The first wrong reading puts P10 on the low value, which is the cumulative reading and the reverse of the exceedance meaning in which P90 is the Low case; a scenario takes no P-label either way.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 32, 'Before EC3-0 the Scenario Builder''s results panel printed ISIALA''s 109.8980 under "P90 (Conservative)". What had it done?', '["Printed the engine''s p90 key, a plain 90th percentile of the sampled NPVs, under an exceedance label, so the conservative card held the High case P10.", "Nothing wrong, since the p90 key names the value the NPV exceeds with 90 percent probability and 109.8980 is ISIALA''s Low case.", "Swapped the mean and the median, since 109.8980 is the sampled emv of the run and 81.1835 is the deterministic value at the typed inputs.", "Used the breakeven percentile rule where the screening rule belongs, which moves the lower card of the sample from 48.7439 to 109.8980."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The first wrong reading takes the engine key p90 as a P-label, the misuse the swap shipped; the Low case P90 is the p10 key, 48.7439, and the emv is 80.1707.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 33, 'Why does the Scenario Builder''s Low case P90 of 48.7439 sit so far above ISIALA''s Low scenario of -72.1531?', '["The Monte Carlo also samples opex, royalty and tax, and their favourable draws lift the low tail of the sample above the scenario''s corner.", "The sample moves each input up to 20 percent from the base while the scenario moves each by 30 percent, so the scenario reaches further out.", "The sample moves price, capex and reserves together once per run, so its low tail is a milder version of the scenario''s same corner.", "The Monte Carlo draws price, capex and reserves independently, one draw per year each, so a bad draw is usually offset; the scenario forces every input bad at once."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The scenario lands well below even the lowest of the 1000 sampled NPVs, 16.3054; opex is never sampled, and the scenario multipliers are 0.8 and 1.2.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 34, 'Working ISIALA by hand, a candidate writes a 2029 tax above the engine''s 19.3660. Which slip does the capstone method name for that?', '["Capex left out of the base, since 2029 should still deduct the second 90.0000 of capex spent in 2028 against that year''s revenue.", "Royalty left in the base: 35 percent of 87.0580 less 13.0587 less 18.6679 is 19.3660, and keeping the 13.0587 in inflates it.", "A discount factor applied to the tax line, which divides the 2029 base by the mid-year factor of 1.327532 before the rate is charged.", "Variable opex taken at the 2027 volume of 1606000.0000 bbl, since an opex figure that is too high raises the taxable base."]'::jsonb, 1, 'No loss or capex is carried into 2029, so its base is that year''s own; royalty 13.0587 and tax 19.3660 make govTake 32.4247 in 2029.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 35, 'Which closing check on ISIALA''s lifetime totals does the capstone method run?', '["864.1699 less govTake 265.6562 and capex 180.0000 leaves 81.0464, the NPV, since the NPV is what the contractor keeps.", "864.1699 less royalty, opex, tax, capex and the depreciation column as well leaves 208.0250, the closing cumulative.", "864.1699 less royalty 129.6255, capex 180.0000, opex 210.4887 and tax 136.0307 leaves 208.0250, the closing cumulative.", "864.1699 less totalGovTake 265.6562 leaves the contractor''s discounted share, which must equal the NPV of 81.0464 plus capex."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The totals close the same way every row does; the depreciation column changes only tax, and 208.0250 is undiscounted where the NPV of 81.0464 is not.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 36, 'ISIALA''s capex belief is stated as 150, 180 and 220 million USD. How does the course write it?', '["A 10th percentile of 150, a median of 180 and a 90th percentile of 220, fitted to a triangular from 127.2260 to 252.3607.", "As P90 capex of 150, P50 of 180 and P10 of 220, the exceedance labels every uncertain quantity in a model takes.", "As a minimum of 150, a mode of 180 and a maximum of 220, since three stated values of a belief describe the corners of a triangle directly.", "As P10 capex of 150, P50 of 180 and P90 of 220, since for a cost the cumulative labels read the right way round."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A parameter takes percentile words and never a P-label, which is the misuse two of the wrong readings make; read as minimum, mode and maximum the belief returns 164.4914 and 203.2668 at its outer quantiles and the tails vanish.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 37, 'ISIALA''s sampled breakeven over 5000 iterations returns 62.1713, 73.3297 and 85.5912 USD per bbl. How are they reported?', '["As a Low case P90 of 62.1713, a Best case P50 of 73.3297 and a High case P10 of 85.5912, as for any outcome of a model.", "As P10 62.1713, P50 73.3297 and P90 85.5912, since for a price the labels simply follow the sorted order of the sample.", "As a Base of 62.1713, a median of 73.3297 and an upside of 85.5912, spread around the stated-median breakeven of 71.6277.", "As the 10th percentile, median and 90th percentile of breakeven price, because a higher breakeven is worse."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A breakeven price takes no P-label at all, which two of the wrong readings apply; the base case at the stated medians is 71.6277, a single run and not a percentile.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 38, 'Which refusal of the screening engine does ISIALA''s Low scenario NPV of -72.1531 inherit that a real operator would not accept?', '["No royalty in loss years, so Low''s value leaves out the 82.9603 of royalty that a real regime would have collected.", "A loss carried forward, which Low uses to shelter its later tax although a real regime taxing only positive bases would forbid it.", "No economic limit: Low still produces and charges all twenty years of the fixed life, including any year that loses money.", "A development year with no revenue, which the quick form inserts into Low to hold its 216.0000 of capex and leaves out of Base."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Royalty is charged in every producing year and no loss is ever carried; the scenario keeps the engine''s limits, which is also why its payback reads 20.0000.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 39, 'The Scenario Builder''s Monte Carlo has a reserves range. What does it actually sample on ISIALA?', '["A total reserves figure from which the engine rederives the life, so a low draw shortens the 20 year case and a high one extends it.", "Each year''s volume, drawn around its base value one year at a time; there is no reserves field and the decline itself is never sampled.", "The decline rate, drawn once per run within 20 percent of 12 percent, which then reshapes the whole profile from 2028 onward.", "The initial rate of 4400 bopd, drawn once per run, with every later year then following the fixed year on year decline of 0.880000 from that draw."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A steeper decline from the same first year is a case it never samples; volume is whatever an initial rate and a decline add up to over twenty rows.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 40, 'Adding a constant gas price lifts the published 10 year base case''s 2028 grossRevenue from 547.5000 to 585.8250. ISIALA''s quick case also shows a gas price in every row. What does gas add to ISIALA''s ledger?', '["Nothing: gas volume is 0, so 2027''s grossRevenue of 112.4200 is 1606000.0000 bbl at 70.0000 over 1e6, oil alone.", "A small gas revenue folded into grossRevenue each year, which is why 2027''s figure sits a little above oil volume times price.", "A credit booked against opex rather than revenue, which lowers the ledger opex column below the fixed and variable halves.", "Gas revenue whenever the Monte Carlo draws on the price range, since that range scales the gas price along with the oil price."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The quick form always sets gas volume to 0 at a gas price of 3.5000, so the column exists and earns nothing; the base case gains revenue only because its gas stream carries a volume.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 41, 'The published tr_missing_profiles case shows grossRevenue 0.0000, opex 5.0000 and net cash flow -5.0000 in both 2032 and 2033. What did the engine do?', '["Ended the case at the last producing year, since a profile that runs out acts as the economic limit the quick form otherwise lacks.", "Refunded tax on each negative base at the case''s tax rate, so part of each 5.0000 loss is carried by the government through a negative tax line.", "Carried each negative base back to 2031 and reduced that year''s tax of 4.9800 against it, which is why 2032 and 2033 both show no tax of their own.", "Read the missing profile as zero, kept charging opex and returned tax of 0.0000 on the negative base, so the contractor carries each loss."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine does not notice the field stopped; its `|| 0` reads a shorter profile as zero, and a negative base produces no refund, no credit and no memory.'),
  ('uncertainty', 'beginner', 'final', null, 42, 'Scale irr_known_21pct''s net cash flows of -100 and +121 down to order 1e-7 million USD. The root is still 21 percent, yet the engine''s IRR moves from 21.0000 to 10.0000. What happened?', '["Flows that small round to zero at four decimals, so the ledger shows no sign change and the solver returns its fixed rate.", "An absolute guard on the slope, |dNPV/dr| below 1e-5, fires on the first iteration and hands back the 10 percent starting guess.", "Mid-year discounting moves the root from 21 to 10 on small flows, because the half-year exponent of (1 + r)^-0.5 dominates the discounting when the cash is tiny.", "Newton converged on the second root of the case at 10 percent, since cash flows that small always produce two roots, as irr_two_roots does."]'::jsonb, 1, 'That scaled case is irr_tiny_cash_flows_derivative_guard, a disagreement recorded in FINDINGS-fiscal.md; the guard is absolute, so it fires on size alone, and a rounding to zero would have returned 0.');
